AB  - Effects of hydrodynamic cavitation (HC) processing on the physicochemical properties of sugarcane (Sachharum officinarum) juice were investigated at varied pressures (5–15 psi) and treatment times (5–30 min). pH, total soluble solids (TSS), viscosity, titratable acidity, reducing sugars, and total color difference in control and HC treated juice were optimized based on vitamin-C, polyphenols, antioxidant activity, and polyphenol oxidase enzyme inactivation. Optimal HC conditions were found at 12.69 psi for 9.77 min. TSS, pH, titratable acidity, and reducing sugar remained unchanged in control and HC processed juice. A 9.52 % reduction in PPO in juice was recorded at optimal HC treatment with 95 % antioxidant activity and 93 % vitamin-C retention. Cavitated samples showed improved physical properties during 14 days storage. This study demonstrated that HC could enhance physiochemical attributes by retaining more bioactives and greater antioxidant activity due to its effect of increased localized pressure and temperature. © 2024 Elsevier B.V.
