AB  - The fouling and flux decline in ultrafiltration (UF) membranes are influenced by various factors that include membrane properties like pore size, hydrophobicity, membrane type, feed properties like solute concentration, particle size, and operating parameters like pH, temperature, and pressure. The ultrafiltration membranes were prepared using cellulose acetate, polysulfone, polyethersulfone, and polyvinylidene fluoride for altering particle size, and membrane properties by incorporating different modifiers or fillers. Hermia model was applied to discover the membrane fouling for prepared membranes, precisely for envisaging the permeate flux variations over time during ultrafiltration Hermia's model parameters k and R2 were estimated by a non-linear optimization procedure. The Artificial neural network (ANN) was used to predict the relationships between input parameters (temperature, transmembrane pressure, and operating time) and output responses of UF membrane behavior (membrane flux and fouling). The fitting accuracy studies were carried out in MATLAB (R) R2020b using the Levenberg-Marquardt backpropagation algorithm. The predicted values from the Hermia and artificial neural network models are then compared with actual experimental data for accuracy. The study demonstrated that ANN modelling, trained using the Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm, achieves a prediction error as low as 2-4 %, outperforming Hermia's model (up to 12 % error). These findings support the use of ANN as a robust tool for the prediction and optimization of altered polymer membrane materials in UF separation process.
